    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **Who this is for:** Developers and Testers\n> **Stages covered:** Stage 4 (Playwright Spec), Stage 5 (BrowserStack Execution), Stage 6 (Defect Triage & Fix Branch)\n> **Prerequisite:** Zephyr test cases must be in **Ready for Execution** status before the spec is written. See [Guide 2](../277708807) if that step has not been completed.\n\n---\n\nWhat This Guide Covers\n----------------------\n\nThis guide covers everything from writing the Playwright spec through to a failed test resulting in a merged fix. It explains the execution matrix, how BrowserStack Automate connects to the CI pipeline, how results sync back to Zephyr Scale, and how the defect triage agent classifies failures and raises fix branches.\n\n---\n\nStage 4 — Writing the Playwright Spec\n-------------------------------------\n\n### Where Specs Live\n\nAll Zimmermann test specs live in:\n\n```\ne2e/tests/zimm/[feature-area]/[STORY-KEY]-[description].[priority].spec.js\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```\ne2e/tests/zimm/checkout/ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\ne2e/tests/zimm/pdp/ZIM-031-variant-selection.p1.spec.js\n```\n\nThe `.p0` / `.p1` suffix determines which CI gate picks up the spec:\n- **`.p0`** — runs in the Digital SIT gate on every merge to `develop`\n- **`.p1`** — runs in the full BrowserStack matrix on PRs to `release/**`\n\n### Selectors — Page Object Model\n\nSelectors must never be written inline in the spec file. They live in the Page Object file for that feature area:\n\n```\ne2e/support/pages/CheckoutPage.js\ne2e/support/pages/ProductPage.js\ne2e/support/pages/AccountPage.js\n```\n\nSelector priority (in order — use the highest available):\n1. ARIA role + accessible name: `page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Place Order' })`\n2. Label: `page.getByLabel('Email address')`\n3. Visible text: `page.getByText('Order confirmed')`\n4. `data-testid` attribute: `page.getByTestId('order-number')`\n5. Never: CSS class, DOM position, tag name alone\n\nThis doubles as a continuous accessibility check — if you cannot select an element by role or label, that element likely has an accessibility problem worth flagging.\n\n### Multi-Region Fixtures\n\nEvery spec is parameterised by `SITE_ID` — not hardcoded to one storefront. The site fixture supplies the correct `baseUrl`, currency, checkout owner, and consent defaults.\n\n```\nimport { SITE_CONFIGS, activeSiteKey } from '../../fixtures/site'\n\nconst site = SITE_CONFIGS[activeSiteKey()]\n\ntest.describe(`Guest checkout — ${site.name}`, () => {\n  test('AC-01: Guest completes checkout with valid card', async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.goto(`${site.baseUrl}/cart`)\n    // ...\n  })\n})\n```\n\nRun a spec against a specific storefront locally:\n\n```\nSITE_ID=uk npx playwright test ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\n```\n\n### Generating the Spec from Zephyr Test Cases\n\nUse Claude Desktop with the MCP connected to pull the test cases directly from Zephyr — no copy-pasting required.\n\n**Step 1 — Ask Claude to retrieve the Zephyr test cases for the story:**\n\n```\nUsing the Atlassian MCP, retrieve all Zephyr test cases linked to Jira story ZIM-042 \nand show me the full detail of each one — steps, expected results, priority, and labels.\n```\n\nClaude will fetch and display all test cases linked to that story. Confirm the list looks right and that all cases are in Ready for Execution status before continuing.\n\n**Step 2 — Ask Claude to generate the Playwright spec:**\n\n```\nUsing those Zephyr test cases, generate a Playwright spec scaffold that follows \nthe Zimmermann e2e conventions:\n- File path: e2e/tests/zimm/checkout/ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\n- Page Object Model — all selectors go in CheckoutPage.js, not inline in the spec\n- Parameterised by SITE_ID using the site fixture\n- Test names must match the Zephyr test case names exactly\n- One test per Zephyr test case\n```\n\nClaude generates the scaffold from the test cases it already retrieved. The spec will have placeholder selectors (e.g. `// TODO: add to CheckoutPage.js`) — the Developer's job is to replace these with correct selectors in the Page Object file, then confirm the spec passes locally against the AU storefront before committing.\n\n**If you need to adjust the output**, give Claude specific instructions:\n\n| What needs changing | What to say |\n| --- | --- |\n| A test step needs a more specific selector hint | \"In the test for TC-03, the payment submission step — note that the button selector should use role 'button' with name 'Place Order'\" |\n| A test should be P0 not P1 | \"Change the file suffix to .p0 — this is a smoke scenario\" |\n| The Page Object file doesn't exist yet | \"Also generate a skeleton CheckoutPage.js with placeholder methods for each action in the spec\" |\n| A test case covers multiple regions | \"TC-05 is labelled ALL — wrap its describe block to iterate across all seven SITE\\_IDs from the fixture\" |\n\nClaude keeps the Zephyr test case detail in context, so corrections are fast and targeted.\n\n---\n\nStage 5 — BrowserStack Execution\n--------------------------------\n\n### When BrowserStack Runs\n\nBrowserStack Automate is triggered by GitHub Actions on **PRs to `release/**`**. It does not run on every `develop` merge — the SIT gate handles that using headless Playwright directly.\n\nThe CI job is defined in `.github/workflows/qa.yml`.\n\n### The Execution Matrix\n\nEvery spec tagged `.p1` or above runs across:\n\n| Storefronts (7) | Browsers (4) |\n| --- | --- |\n| AU | Chrome (latest) |\n| UK | Safari (latest) |\n| US Domestic | Firefox (latest) |\n| US International | Edge (latest) |\n| EU Domestic |  |\n| EU International |  |\n| ME |  |\n\nThis is 7 × 4 = **28 parallel execution contexts** per spec. What takes a human tester 3 days runs in under 2 hours.\n\n### `.browserstack.yml` Configuration\n\nThe BrowserStack config is scoped to Zimmermann tests only — it does not run the upstream Salesforce test suite:\n\n```\nbrowsers:\n  - browser: chrome\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: safari\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: firefox\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: edge\n    browser_version: latest\n\nplatforms:\n  - os: Windows\n    os_version: 11\n  - os: OS X\n    os_version: Ventura\n\nproject: Zimmermann-SFCC\nbuild: Sprint-${SPRINT_NUMBER}\nname: ${GITHUB_REF_NAME}-${GITHUB_SHA}\n\ntestSuite: e2e/tests/zimm\n```\n\nThe `Sprint-${SPRINT_NUMBER}` build tag makes results traceable back to Zephyr Scale — every BrowserStack session is tagged to the sprint, which maps to the Jira sprint.\n\n### Result Sync to Zephyr Scale\n\nAfter each BrowserStack run completes, the CI pipeline posts results back to Zephyr Scale via the Zephyr Scale API. Each test result — pass, fail, or skip — is recorded against the matching Zephyr test case using the test case name as the link key.\n\n* Pass: test case execution record shows green in Zephyr\n* Fail: test case execution record shows red, with a link to the BrowserStack session recording\n* Skip: test case execution record shows skipped (e.g. if the storefront is not yet live)\n\nThe Tester does not manually update Zephyr execution records — the sync is automatic.\n\n---\n\nStage 6 — Defect Triage & Fix Branch\n------------------------------------\n\n### How Failures Are Classified\n\nWhen a BrowserStack run produces failures, the triage agent runs against the failure report and classifies each failure into one of three categories:\n\n| Classification | Meaning | Agent Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Code defect** | The application is not behaving as specified — a bug in the Zimmermann codebase | Agent creates a fix branch, investigates, implements a fix (Tier 2–4 files only), raises PR |\n| **Environment issue** | Test failed due to a missing environment dependency — wrong test data, service unavailable, sandbox not seeded | Agent raises a Jira defect with classification \"Environment\" and assigns to the relevant team |\n| **Test data issue** | Test failed because a fixture value is stale or incorrect — e.g. a product SKU that no longer exists in the test environment | Agent raises a Jira defect with classification \"Test Data\" and assigns to the Tester who owns the fixture |\n\n### The Fix Branch Model\n\nFor code defects, the agent's behaviour is tier-gated:\n\n| File Tier | Agent Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Tier 1** — design tokens, shared hooks, runtime config, CI workflows | Agent **stops**. Raises a Jira defect tagged Tier 1 and assigns to the Lead Developer. No automated fix attempt. |\n| **Tier 2** — global shell components, shared product components, hooks with 5+ consumers | Agent creates a fix branch and raises a PR, but adds a Tier 2 warning label and requests a second reviewer |\n| **Tier 3–4** — squad-owned and feature-scoped files | Agent creates a fix branch, investigates, implements a fix, and raises a PR with full investigation detail |\n\n**In all cases: no PR is merged without human code review.** The agent can never merge its own PR. This is enforced by GitHub branch protection rules on `develop` and all `release/**` branches.\n\n### What the Agent Raises in Jira\n\nFor every failure (regardless of classification), the agent creates a Jira defect with:\n\n* **Summary:** `[STORY-KEY] [Test Case Name] — [Failure classification] — [Browser/Storefront]`\n* **Description:** The full failure message, stack trace, and a link to the BrowserStack session recording\n* **Labels:** `defect`, the classification (`code-defect` / `env-issue` / `test-data`), the affected storefront, and the severity\n* **Severity:** P1 (blocks story sign-off) or P2 (does not block but must be resolved before release)\n* **Link to:** the original Jira story and the Zephyr test case\n\n### What the Agent Raises in GitHub\n\nFor code defects on Tier 2–4 files, the PR raised by the agent includes:\n\n* **Branch name:** `fix/agent-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]`\n* **PR title:** `[STORY-KEY] Fix: [Test case name] — [Browser/Storefront]`\n* **PR body:** root cause analysis, files changed and why, link to the Zephyr test case, link to the BrowserStack session, file tier confirmation, and (for Tier 2) a note that a second reviewer is required\n\n### Reviewing and Merging Agent PRs\n\nThe Developer assigned to review an agent PR must:\n\n1. Read the root cause analysis — confirm it makes sense\n2. Review the code change — does it correctly fix the failure without introducing new problems?\n3. Check that only the expected files were changed — the agent should not have touched Tier 1 files or files outside the failure's scope\n4. Run the failing test locally against the fix branch to confirm it passes\n5. Merge if satisfied — or close the PR and fix manually if the agent's approach is wrong\n\nIf the agent's fix is wrong, close the PR, add a comment explaining what the agent got wrong, and fix manually. The incorrect fix attempt is useful context — do not delete it.\n\n---\n\nSeverity Reference\n------------------\n\n| Severity | Meaning | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P0 | Blocks smoke — checkout or core journey completely broken | Immediate fix required before any deployment |\n| P1 | Story cannot be signed off — acceptance criterion fails | Fix before story moves to Done |\n| P2 | Edge case or non-blocking defect | Fix before release candidate is cut |\n| P3 | Cosmetic or low-impact | Backlog for prioritisation |\n\n---\n\nQuick Reference — Stages 4–6\n----------------------------\n\n```\nZephyr test cases: Ready for Execution\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Retrieve all Zephyr test cases linked to ZIM-042\"\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Generate a Playwright spec scaffold from those test cases\"\n        │\n        ▼\nDeveloper adds correct selectors to Page Object file\n        │\n        ▼\nSpec passes locally → PR raised to release/**\n        │\n        ▼\nGitHub Actions triggers BrowserStack\n        │\n        ▼\n28 parallel contexts run (7 storefronts × 4 browsers)\n        │\n        ▼\nResults sync to Zephyr Scale automatically\n        │\n   ┌────┴────┐\n   │         │\n PASS      FAIL\n   │         │\n   ▼         ▼\nStory    Triage agent classifies failure\nmoves    │\nforward  ├─ Environment / Test Data: Jira defect raised, assigned\n         │\n         └─ Code defect:\n              ├─ Tier 1: Jira defect, assigned to Lead Dev\n              └─ Tier 2–4: Fix branch + PR raised by agent\n                    │\n                    ▼\n              Developer reviews PR ◀ HUMAN GATE 3\n                    │\n              Merge or close and fix manually\n```\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "277708807",
    "title": "Guide 2: Creating Zephyr Test Cases via MCP",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/277708807",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **Who this is for:** Business Analysts and Testers\n> **Stage covered:** Stage 3 (Zephyr Test Case Creation)\n> **Prerequisite:** The Jira story must have status **AC Approved** before starting this guide. See [Guide 1](../277577757) if AC has not been written yet.\n\n---\n\nWhat This Guide Covers\n----------------------\n\nThis guide explains how to use the Atlassian MCP (via Claude Desktop) to create structured Zephyr Scale test cases from approved acceptance criteria — and how to link them back to the Jira story so they are traceable through the pipeline.\n\nThe output of Stage 3 is a set of test cases in Zephyr Scale, each linked to the Jira story, reviewed by a Tester, and marked **Ready for Execution**.\n\n---\n\nBefore You Start\n----------------\n\nYou need:\n- Claude Desktop with the Atlassian MCP connected ([setup guide](../275251202))\n- The Jira story key (e.g. `ZIM-042`)\n- The approved AC from the Jira story (copy from the Acceptance Criteria field)\n- Access to Zephyr Scale in Jira (ask your Squad Lead if you do not see the Zephyr menu)\n\n---\n\nWhat Is Zephyr Scale?\n---------------------\n\nZephyr Scale is the test case management tool inside Jira. It stores test cases as structured records with individual test steps, expected results, priority, and execution status. Test cases link to Jira stories so you can see at a glance which stories have test coverage, and which test cases have passed or failed against a given release.\n\nWhen a BrowserStack automated run completes (Stage 5), results sync back to Zephyr automatically — so the execution record is always tied to the test case, not just a log file.\n\n---\n\nThe MCP Workflow — Step by Step\n-------------------------------\n\n### Step 1 — Open Claude Desktop and confirm MCP is connected\n\nOpen Claude Desktop. At the bottom of a new chat window you should see a 🔨 icon with a number (the count of available MCP tools). If you do not see it, check the [MCP setup guide](../275251202).\n\n### Step 2 — Retrieve the story AC via MCP\n\nAsk Claude to pull the story directly from Jira:\n\n```\nUsing the Atlassian MCP, retrieve the Jira story ZIM-042 and show me the \nAcceptance Criteria field and the full description.\n```\n\nClaude will fetch the story and display the content. Confirm the AC shown matches the approved version before proceeding.\n\n### Step 3 — Ask Claude to generate the Zephyr test cases\n\nClaude already has the story in context from Step 2. You do not need to copy-paste or fill in a template — just ask it directly:\n\n```\nUsing the AC from that story, generate a structured Zephyr Scale test case \nfor each acceptance criterion.\n```\n\nThat is enough for most stories. Claude knows the Zimmermann project conventions from context and will produce one test case per AC item, with steps, expected results, priority, and region labels.\n\n**If you want to give Claude more control over specific things**, you can add one or two short instructions:\n\n```\nUsing the AC from that story, generate a structured Zephyr Scale test case \nfor each acceptance criterion. \n\nMake the test steps specific enough to automate — name the exact button or \nfield the tester would interact with, not just \"proceed to the next step\".\n```\n\nOr, if you know certain scenarios need to be flagged as P0:\n\n```\nUsing the AC from that story, generate a structured Zephyr Scale test case \nfor each acceptance criterion. Mark AC-01 and AC-02 as Critical priority — \nthey are P0 smoke scenarios.\n```\n\n**If the output needs adjusting**, tell Claude specifically what to fix rather than starting again:\n\n| What needs changing | What to say |\n| --- | --- |\n| A step is too vague | \"Step 4 on TC-02 — be more specific about what the tester clicks to submit payment\" |\n| A test case is missing an edge case | \"TC-03 is missing the case where Didomi consent has not been given — add a step and expected result for that\" |\n| Region tag is wrong | \"TC-05 applies to EU DOM and EU INTL, not just AU — update the label\" |\n| Two test cases should be one | \"Merge TC-06 and TC-07 — they are testing the same scenario\" |\n| A test case should be split | \"TC-08 is covering two different error paths — split it into two separate test cases\" |\n\nClaude keeps the full context of the conversation, so corrections are fast — you do not need to re-paste anything.\n\n### Step 4 — Review Claude's output before creating anything in Zephyr\n\nClaude's output is a draft. **Do not create anything in Zephyr until you have reviewed it.**\n\nWork through each test case and check:\n\n* **Test steps are actionable** — \"Click the Place Order button\" is actionable. \"Complete checkout\" is not.\n* **Expected results are observable** — \"The error message 'Your card was declined' appears below the card number field\" is observable. \"An error shows\" is not.\n* **Priority is appropriate** — happy path should always be High or Critical.\n* **Labels match the region** — an AU-only AC item should not be tagged ALL.\n* **Step count is proportionate** — a checkout flow should have 8–15 steps. Fewer usually means detail is missing.\n\nWhen you are satisfied, move to Step 5.\n\n### Step 5 — Create the test cases in Zephyr via MCP\n\nAsk Claude to create the reviewed test cases directly in Zephyr:\n\n```\nCreate these test cases in Zephyr Scale for project SFCC, linked to Jira \nstory ZIM-042. Create them one at a time and confirm each one before \nproceeding to the next.\n```\n\nClaude will use the MCP tools to create each test case and will report back the test case ID for each one. Note these IDs — you will need them in the next step.\n\n### Step 6 — Confirm the link to the Jira story\n\nAfter all test cases are created, ask Claude to verify they are linked:\n\n```\nConfirm that all the test cases you just created are linked to Jira story ZIM-042.\n```\n\nIf any are not linked, ask Claude to add the link.\n\n### Step 7 — Mark test cases as Ready for Execution\n\nIn Zephyr Scale (via Jira), open each test case and set the status to **Ready for Execution**. This is the human gate — the Tester is confirming the test case is correct and ready to be run.\n\nDo not mark a test case Ready if you have not reviewed it. The status signals to the Developer and CI pipeline that this test case is safe to automate.\n\n---\n\nZephyr Test Case — What Good Looks Like\n---------------------------------------\n\n```\nTest Case Name: ZIM-042-TC-01: Guest completes checkout with a valid card [AU]\nPriority: High\nLabels: AU\nLink to Story: ZIM-042\n\nPrecondition:\n- Guest shopper is on the zimmermann.com AU storefront\n- One or more items are in the bag\n- Shopper has NOT previously accepted or declined Didomi consent for analytics\n\nTest Steps:\nStep 1: Navigate to the bag page\n  Expected: Bag page loads showing all added items with correct prices in AUD\n\nStep 2: Click \"Checkout\"\n  Expected: Checkout page loads at the Contact step\n\nStep 3: Enter a valid email address and click Continue\n  Expected: Shipping Address step becomes active\n\nStep 4: Enter a valid Australian shipping address and click Continue\n  Expected: Shipping Method step becomes active\n\nStep 5: Select a shipping method and click Continue\n  Expected: Payment step becomes active\n\nStep 6: Enter valid Adyen test card details and click Place Order\n  Expected: Request is submitted to Adyen\n\nStep 7: Observe the outcome\n  Expected: Order confirmation page loads displaying an order number\n  And: Order confirmation email is triggered (verify in test inbox)\n\nOverall Expected Result:\nOrder is created successfully. Confirmation page renders with order number. \nConfirmation email triggers. No GTM purchase event fires (Didomi consent not given).\n```\n\n---\n\nNaming Conventions\n------------------\n\n| Field | Convention | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Test case name | `[STORY KEY]-TC-[N]: [Descriptive title] [REGION]` | `ZIM-042-TC-03: Card declined at payment step [AU]` |\n| Labels | Region tag from AC, uppercase | `AU`, `UK`, `ALL`, `EU-DOM` |\n| Priority | Critical (P0 smoke), High (happy path + core errors), Medium (edge cases), Low (cosmetic) |  |\n| Folder in Zephyr | Match the story's epic or feature area | `Checkout / Guest Checkout` |\n\n---\n\nCommon Mistakes\n---------------\n\n| Mistake | Fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| Creating test cases before the BA has approved AC | Check story status — it must be **AC Approved** first |\n| Test steps that are too vague to automate | Tell Claude: \"be more specific — name the exact button or field\" |\n| Skipping the review step | Review is mandatory — Claude's first output is a draft, not a finished test case |\n| Not linking the test case to the Jira story | Every test case must be linked — this is how BrowserStack results trace back to the story |\n| Marking Ready for Execution without reading the test case | This is the human gate — the Tester is accountable for cases they mark Ready |\n\n---\n\nQuick Reference — Stage 3 Flow\n------------------------------\n\n```\nStory status: AC Approved\n        │\n        ▼\nOpen Claude Desktop — confirm MCP connected\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Retrieve Jira story ZIM-042 and show me the AC\"\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Using that AC, generate Zephyr test cases\"\n        │\n        ▼\nReview output — give Claude specific corrections if needed\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Create these test cases in Zephyr, linked to ZIM-042\"\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Confirm all test cases are linked to ZIM-042\"\n        │\n        ▼\nTester marks test cases Ready for Execution ◀ HUMAN GATE 2\n        │\n        ▼\nStory ready for Stage 4 — Playwright spec authoring\n```\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f4cb",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "305528833",
    "title": "Guide 4: E2E Testing — Playwright &amp; Gherkin Repo Conventions",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305528833",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This is the **developer-facing engineering reference** for the E2E Playwright layer in the `zimmermann-pwa` repo. For the broader BA/Tester process this plugs into (story intake → AC → Zephyr test cases → this layer → BrowserStack → defect triage), see [AI-Assisted QA — Overview & Methodology](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/277544962) and Guides 1–3.\n\nScope\n-----\n\nThis guide covers the **E2E layer** only. It is additive to — not a replacement for — the unit and component-interaction layers:\n\n| Layer | Tooling | Lives in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit (pure logic) | Vitest | `*.test.ts` alongside the source |\n| Component interaction | Storybook `play` functions | `*.stories.tsx` alongside the component |\n| End-to-end user journeys | Playwright + Gherkin | `e2e/` at the repo root (this guide) |\n\nModel\n-----\n\nHuman-in-the-lead. Claude Code generates tests — a Lead QA validates them.\n\n**Rule:** never treat test generation as complete until the Lead QA has approved the `.feature` file.\n\nDefaultnoneDeveloper writes story →\n/qa-generate produces .feature file →\nLead QA validates / annotates →\nDeveloper runs /qa-generate again →\nPlaywright .spec.ts is generated →\nTest is committed\n\nDirectory layout\n----------------\n\nDefaultnonee2e/\n├── features/ # Gherkin .feature files — source of truth\n│ ├── checkout/\n│ ├── search/\n│ ├── account/\n│ ├── integrations/\n│ └── regression/ # Magento baseline parity tests\n├── fixtures/ # Test data (multi-region aware)\n│ ├── site.ts # Regional site configs (AU, US, UK, EU, ME, HK)\n│ ├── customers.ts # Guest and registered customer personas\n│ ├── products.ts # Product test data with regional pricing\n│ └── orders.ts # Order fixtures for history/confirmation tests\n├── support/\n│ ├── pages/ # Page Object Model — one file per SFCC page\n│ │ ├── basePage.ts\n│ │ ├── checkoutPage.ts\n│ │ └── productPage.ts\n│ └── helpers/\n│ └── auth.ts # Guest and registered auth helpers\n└── tests/\n└── zimm/ # Zimmermann Playwright tests (generated from .feature files)\n├── checkout/\n├── search/\n├── account/\n└── integrations/\n\nThis is a slim overrides project, not a fork of the upstream PWA Kit monorepo — there is no `e2e/tests/desktop/` or `e2e/tests/mobile/` Salesforce scaffold to preserve.\n\nWriting a feature file\n----------------------\n\n* One `.feature` file per Jira story\n* File name: `{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}.feature`\n* Every file needs: Jira tag (`@SFCC-NNN`), area tag (`@checkout` etc.), and `@p0` if it's a critical path\n* At least one happy path and one failure/edge scenario\n* Plain English only — no selectors, no code, no technical detail\n\nGenerating tests with /qa-generate\n----------------------------------\n\nIn a Claude Code session: `/qa-generate`. The skill asks for Jira story details, drafts the `.feature` file, pauses for Lead QA validation, then generates the Playwright spec and any required Page Object additions.\n\nOnly run this on a feature branch. Never on `develop` or `main`.\n\nPage Object Model — selector priority\n-------------------------------------\n\nEnforced in code review:\n\n1. **Accessible selectors** — role + accessible name, label, visible text (`getByRole`, `getByLabelText`, `getByText`). First choice.\n2. **Semantic HTML attributes** — `getByAltText`, `getByTitle`, `getByPlaceholderText`.\n3. **`data-testid`** — last resort only, for elements with no semantic meaning.\n\nSelectors live in `e2e/support/pages/` only. Never write selectors directly in test files.\n\nMulti-region test fixtures\n--------------------------\n\nZimmermann serves 6 regions (AU, US, UK, EU, ME, HK) across **7 SFCC sites** (AU, UK, US, EU domestic, EU international, HK, ME — US confirmed as a single site, REG-005). All test data must be region-aware from day one — keyed by `SITE_ID` rather than retrofitted.\n\n> Open item: `config/sites.js` is still the OOTB Salesforce default and must be customised for the 6-site setup before regional fixtures can be wired up end-to-end — tracked on the [7.1c CI/CD & Environment Setup Checklist](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305430530) page.\n\nDefaultbash# Default region (au)\nnpx playwright test e2e/tests/zimm\n# Specific region\nSITE\\_ID=us npx playwright test e2e/tests/zimm\n\nRunning tests locally\n---------------------\n\nDefaultbash# P0 suite only (what the Digital SIT gate runs)\nnpm run test:sit\n# All Zimmermann Playwright tests\nnpx playwright test e2e/tests/zimm\n# Single test file\nnpx playwright test e2e/tests/zimm/checkout/SFCC-000\\_guest-checkout.p0.spec.ts\n\nSet `RETAIL_APP_HOME` to your local dev server or SIT URL in `.env` at the project root.\n\nMagento baseline regression\n---------------------------\n\nFiles in `e2e/features/regression/` document expected Magento 2 behaviour.\n\nWhen a baseline test fails against SFCC: do not update the test to pass; add `# DELTA: SFCC behaviour differs — [description]`; raise with Lead QA — they classify: bug, intentional change, or platform constraint.\n\nQA annotation workflow\n----------------------\n\nWhen Lead QA annotates a correction in a `.feature` file:\n\nDefaultnone# QA ANNOTATION: Step 2 is wrong. Zimmermann checkout shows the order summary\n# BEFORE payment entry, not after. Update this scenario.\n\nResponse: update the `.feature` file to reflect the corrected behaviour, run `/qa-generate` to regenerate the Playwright spec, update the Page Object only if a new element is referenced, confirm the change.\n\nBrowserStack cross-browser runs\n-------------------------------\n\nBrowserStack runs automatically on PRs targeting `release/**` branches via `qa.yml`. Browsers tested: Chrome (OS X), Safari (OS X), Firefox (Windows), Edge (Windows). Full defect-triage flow for BrowserStack failures: see [Guide 3: BrowserStack Execution, Result Sync & Defect Triage](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/277676053).\n\nTo trigger a run manually: Actions → QA → Run workflow → select branch. Results appear in the BrowserStack Automate dashboard and Zephyr (via BrowserStack Test Observability — confirm integration with QA Lead).\n\nSecrets required\n----------------\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `BROWSERSTACK_USERNAME` | BrowserStack account |\n| `BROWSERSTACK_ACCESS_KEY` | BrowserStack account |\n| `RETAIL_APP_HOME_SIT` | SIT storefront URL |\n| `PWA_E2E_USER_EMAIL` | Registered test account email |\n| `PWA_E2E_USER_PASSWORD` | Registered test account password |\n| `E2E_STANDARD_PRODUCT_SKU` | In-stock product for checkout tests |\n| `E2E_VARIANT_PRODUCT_SKU` | Multi-variant product for PDP tests |\n| `E2E_OOS_PRODUCT_SKU` | Out-of-stock product |\n| `E2E_SALE_PRODUCT_SKU` | Sale product for price display tests |\n| `E2E_FIXTURE_ORDER_NUMBER` | Completed order for history tests |\n\nSee the [SIT Quality Gates — Thresholds & Troubleshooting](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840754) page for one-time GitHub Secrets setup.\n\nWhat NOT to do\n--------------\n\n* Never write selectors directly in test files — always use Page Objects\n* Never hardcode test data in test files — always use `e2e/fixtures/`\n* Never update a baseline regression test to pass without Lead QA sign-off\n* Never generate a Playwright test before the `.feature` file has been validated\n* Never add `@skip` without a Jira ticket reference and Lead QA approval\n* Never use `page.waitForTimeout()` — use `waitForSelector` or `waitForResponse`\n* Never generate tests that only cover the happy path",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "261750785",
    "title": "Development Standards",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "General day-to-day coding conventions for the Zimmermann PWA Kit. For the pattern used to override a base Salesforce component vs. build a new Zimmermann-specific one, see [Component Override Strategy](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261881857).\n\nNaming conventions\n------------------\n\n| What | Convention | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| New Zimmermann files and folders | `camelCase` | `backInStockButton/`, `useWishlist.ts` |\n| Overrides of Salesforce base files | Keep upstream kebab-case exactly | `product-tile/index.tsx` |\n| Component named export | Matches folder name | `BackInStockButton` exported from `backInStockButton/` |\n| Component props type | Component name + `Props` | `BackInStockButtonProps` |\n| Hook files | `.ts` (not `.tsx`) | `useWishlist.ts` |\n| Hook export | Matches filename | `useWishlist` |\n| Callback props | `onEventName` | `onAddToCart`, `onClose` |\n| Identifiers and copy | Australian spelling | `colour`, `centre`, `organisation` |\n| Override files | Keep upstream Salesforce spelling verbatim | — |\n\nTypeScript only inside `overrides/`. No `.js` or `.jsx` files. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` before finishing any task that touches `overrides/`.\n\nNo `any` without an inline comment explaining why a narrower type is impossible.\n\nPrefer `type` over `interface` for component props and shared shapes.\n\nDesign tokens\n-------------\n\nDesign tokens (colours, typography, spacing) live in `overrides/app/theme/foundation/` — **frozen (Tier 1)**, owned by the UI Designer. Do not modify except to sync with Figma; ask the UI Designer if a needed colour/font isn't in the token set.\n\nDefaulttsx// ✅ correct — uses the token\n<Text color=\"brand.primary\" fontSize=\"md\">…</Text>\n// ❌ wrong — hardcoded value\n<Text color=\"#1a1a1a\" fontSize=\"16px\">…</Text>\n\nPer-component theme overrides (button variant, link styles, etc.) live in `overrides/app/theme/components/` — Tier 2 (protected), owned by the UI Designer.\n\nCommon shared components\n------------------------\n\n| Component | Path | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Header | `overrides/app/components/header/` | Tier 2 — coordinate with both squads before touching |\n| Page container | `overrides/app/components/pageContainer/` | Wraps page-level layout |\n| App config | `overrides/app/components/_app-config/` | Tier 2 |\n| Button | `overrides/app/components/button/` | Zimmermann button override |\n\nFor icons, use `@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/icons`. If a required icon is missing, get it from Figma or ask the UI Designer — do not substitute a visually different one.\n\nStyling\n-------\n\n1. Chakra UI props first.\n2. `.scss` file alongside the component only when Chakra props cannot achieve the required result.\n\nNever hardcode hex colours, pixel spacing values, or raw font sizes in component props.\n\nStorybook\n---------\n\nEvery component exported from `overrides/app/components/` or `overrides/app/pages/` must have a `.stories.tsx` file.\n\n| Category | What goes here |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Foundation/` | Design token visualisations |\n| `Elements/` | Generic building blocks (Button, Input, Badge) |\n| `Commerce/` | Commerce-specific components (ProductTile, OrderSummary) |\n| `Layout/` | Global shell (Header, Footer, DrawerMenu) |\n| `Forms/` | Form assemblies (AddressForm, CheckoutContactForm) |\n| `Pages/` | Full page assemblies (PDP, PLP, Cart) |\n\nEvery story file needs a `Default` named export. Add named exports for meaningful states (`Loading`, `OutOfStock`) — not for every prop combination.\n\nTesting\n-------\n\n| Layer | Tool | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Vitest | Data mapping, transforms, hook logic in isolation |\n| Component interaction | Storybook `play` | User interactions (clicks, form fills, focus) |\n| E2E | Playwright | Full user journeys across pages |\n\nVitest tests co-locate with source as `*.test.ts`.\n\nBefore opening a PR\n-------------------\n\nDefaultbashnpx tsc --noEmit # TypeScript must be clean\nnpm run lint # lint must pass\nnpm run test # unit tests must pass\n\nClaude Code's `/code-quality` skill runs a pre-PR audit of changed files; `/pr-checklist` generates a structured PR description.\n\nFile protection tiers\n---------------------\n\nNot all files are equally safe to edit.\n\n| Tier | What | Rule |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Tier 1 — Frozen** | Design tokens, shared commerce hooks, SSR/config, CI/CD | Stop. Get owner approval before any change. |\n| **Tier 2 — Protected** | Global shell, shared product components, translations, shared hooks | PR requires a second reviewer. Notify the owner. |\n| **Unclassified** | A squad's own feature components and pages | Normal feature-branch workflow. |\n\nFull lists live in the repo's `.claude/rules/frozenFiles.md` and `.claude/rules/protectedFiles.md`.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "261881857",
    "title": "Component Override Strategy",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261881857",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "How Zimmermann customisation works on top of the base Salesforce PWA Kit template. For general naming/testing/styling conventions, see the parent [Development Standards](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785) page.\n\nThe overrides pattern\n---------------------\n\nThis project is a **slim overrides project** on top of `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10.\n\nThe base template lives in `node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/`. It cannot and must not be edited — changes there would be wiped out by the next `npm install`. All Zimmermann customisations live in `overrides/app/`, which the PWA Kit module resolution layer checks before falling back to the base template. For example:\n\nDefaultnonenode\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx\n↑ base template — never touch this\noverrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n↑ Zimmermann override — this is what runs in production\n\nIf a file exists in `overrides/app/`, it replaces the base template version entirely. If it does not exist in `overrides/app/`, the base template version runs.\n\nOverriding vs Creating a new file\n---------------------------------\n\n### A. Overriding a base component\n\nUse this when changing a component that already exists in the base template (e.g. `product-tile`, `header`, `checkout`).\n\n1. **Copy the file** — copy the base file to the mirrored path under `overrides/`, convert it to TypeScript, and add a temporary `console.log` to confirm the override is rendering: Defaultbashcp node\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx \\\n   overrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n2. Convert the file to TypeScript (`.js` → `.ts`/`.tsx`), and update import references within the new file.\n3. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` to confirm no TypeScript errors.\n\nTo test the override is working: add `console.log('[overrides] product-tile/index.tsx')` near the top, start the dev server, open the relevant page, and confirm the log fires in the browser console. Remove the `console.log` and make functional changes only after the override is confirmed rendering.\n\n**Keep the filename identical** (kebab-case) — overrides of Salesforce files keep the upstream filename exactly.\n\n### B. Creating a new Zimmermann-specific component\n\nUse this when building something that doesn't exist in the base template (e.g. a \"Back In Stock\" button, a loyalty points badge).\n\nNew custom components go under `overrides/app/components/custom/`, e.g.:\n\nDefaultnoneoverrides/app/components/custom/backInStockButton/\nindex.tsx ← the component\nindex.stories.tsx ← Storybook story (required)\nindex.test.ts ← Vitest (required if the component has non-trivial logic)\n\nNaming: `camelCase` for Zimmermann-authored folders and files (e.g. `backInStockButton/`, `useWishlist.ts`, `formatPrice.ts`).\n\nBranch first\n------------\n\nEvery change goes on a feature branch — never directly on `develop` or `main`.\n\nDefaultbashgit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123\\_short-description\n\nBranch naming: `feature/{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}`. Full branching model: see the [Branching Guide (GitFlow)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733) page under Development Standards & Tooling.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

