  "metadata": {
    "id": "304840708",
    "title": "Environment Topology &amp; Promotion Path",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840708",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 3,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "Six-tier deployment topology for the Zimmermann PWA. NewStore OMS runs as a parallel lane that converges with the Digital lane at STAGE.\n\n> Operational details for the SIT tier — credentials, rollback, contacts — live on [05.6 - Environment Details](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/285540356). Branch rules for developers live in the repo's `AGENTS.md` (Branch Rules section).\n\nDefaultnoneLOCAL → DEV → SIT (parallel) → STAGE → UAT → PROD\n\nZimmermann has 20 SFCC sandboxes available and no MRT environment licence restriction — each MRT tier gets a dedicated environment with no shared-environment contention. Local runs on developer machines via `npm start` only — there is no MRT deployment for feature branches.\n\nTier 1 — LOCAL\n--------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | n/a | Not applicable — no deployment |\n| MRT / PWA | Developer machine (`npm start`) | Local SSR dev server at <http://localhost:3000> — no MRT deployment |\n| 3P systems | All stubs via MSW | No real third-party calls |\n| OMS SIT | Not in scope |  |\n\n* Deploys from: n/a — the developer's machine only.\n* Third-party config: all services mocked via MSW stubs.\n* Logging: debug level. Source maps exposed.\n\nTier 2 — DEV\n------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | SFCC STG org | Jobs: AP21 feed, OCI sync, Lengow |\n| MRT / PWA | MRT staging | 7 storefronts, Playwright matrix, Lighthouse CI |\n| 3P systems | MuleSoft STG only | All other third parties stubbed via MSW |\n| OMS SIT | Not in scope |  |\n\n* Deploys from: every merge to `develop`, auto via CI.\n* Gate — **Digital SIT Gate**: composite required status check. All six signals must be green:\n* Vitest unit tests: all pass\n* Storybook interaction tests: all pass\n* Playwright P0: 100% pass rate across 7 storefront configs\n* Lighthouse CI: Performance ≥ 80, Accessibility ≥ 90, Best Practices ≥ 90; >5 pt trend drop blocked\n* MuleSoft contract tests: no schema breach\n* Bundle size: main.js < 180 kB, vendor.js < 250 kB, main.css < 50 kB\n* CDN / cache: 60s page TTL, 1h asset TTL.\n\nTier 3 — SIT (parallel — never gates Digital)\n---------------------------------------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | Dedicated sandbox | Mirrors STG config — isolated from Digital STG data |\n| MRT / PWA | MRT sit | Auto-receives every `develop` merge after Digital SIT gate passes |\n| 3P systems | NewStore STG, MuleSoft STG, Adyen SBX | Real systems for OMS SIT scope |\n| 3P stubs | Global-e, Narvar | Not in OMS SIT scope |\n| OMS SIT | SIT | NewStore runs 2–3 month SIT cycles here |\n\n* Critical rules: SIT is **not** a Digital gate — Digital promotes independently. SIT does **not** gate STAGE — STAGE opens only when OMS SIT is complete AND Digital SIT is green.\n* Bundle retention: 10 bundles.\n* See [05.6 - Environment Details](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/285540356) for credentials, rollback, and contacts.\n\nTier 4 — STAGE (convergence — time-boxed, Jira-booked)\n------------------------------------------------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | Pre-prod org | Full config, jobs, pricebooks, promotions |\n| MRT / PWA | MRT pre-prod | Full stack, all 3P real, Perf + a11y, visual regression |\n| 3P systems | All systems real | NewStore STG, MuleSoft STG, Adyen SBX, Global-e STG, Narvar STG |\n| OMS SIT | Convergence point | OMS SIT must be **complete** before this window opens |\n\n* Deploys from: manual trigger only — requires a Jira booking ticket approved by the Zimmermann environment owner.\n* Entry criteria (both required): Digital SIT Gate green on the `release/*` branch; NewStore OMS SIT complete (sign-off on the Jira ticket).\n* CDN / cache: 300s page TTL, 24h asset TTL — production-mirrored.\n\nTier 5 — UAT\n------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | UAT org | Merch sign-off, production-mirrored config |\n| MRT / PWA | MRT UAT | Business acceptance testing, all 6 regions |\n| 3P systems | All systems real | Same as STAGE |\n| OMS SIT | Feeds UAT | No further OMS SIT required after STAGE |\n\n* Deploys from: manual trigger — requires business stakeholder sign-off per region.\n\nTier 6 — PRODUCTION\n-------------------\n\n| Layer | Environment | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SFCC BM | PROD org | Live BM config — AU first, then wave rollout |\n| MRT / PWA | MRT Production | AU canary → all regions via `mrt:activate` |\n| 3P systems | All PROD systems | Live credentials, live data |\n| OMS SIT | Feeds PROD | NewStore PROD activated with Digital wave rollout |\n\n* Deploys from: release manager only — manual approval gate in GitHub Environments.\n* Canary: AU goes first; remaining regions open only after error rate < 0.5% over 2h on AU.\n* Rollback: `mrt:activate --target production --bundle-id <PREVIOUS_ID>`. 50-bundle retention (~30-day window).\n\nBranch → Environment → Trigger matrix\n-------------------------------------\n\n| Branch | Tier | Trigger | Gate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `feature/*` | LOCAL (developer machine — no MRT deployment) | `npm start` | None — fast iteration |\n| `develop` | DEV (MRT staging) | Merge to `develop` | Vitest + Storybook interaction tests + Playwright P0 + bundle size + MuleSoft contract tests + Lighthouse CI + LHCI trend gate |\n| `develop` | SIT (MRT sit) | After `sit-gate` passes on `develop` | Digital SIT Gate green |\n| `release/v*.*.*` | STAGE (MRT pre-prod) | Manual | Jira booking confirmed, Digital SIT green, OMS SIT complete |\n| `release/v*.*.*` | UAT (MRT UAT) | Manual | Business stakeholder sign-off |\n| `main` | PROD (MRT Production) | Manual | Release manager approval, post-canary smoke |\n| `hotfix/*` | PROD (expedited) | Merge to `main` | Abbreviated pipeline, approval gate retained |\n\nGitHub Environment secret scope\n-------------------------------\n\n| GitHub Environment | Secrets scoped | Requires reviewer |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `staging` | Dev credentials | No |\n| `sit` | SIT credentials | Yes — Zimmermann environment owner |\n| `pre-prod` | Pre-prod credentials | Yes — Zimmermann environment owner |\n| `uat` | UAT credentials | Yes — Zimmermann stakeholder |\n| `production` | PROD credentials | Yes — Release manager |\n\nSIT secrets must be scoped to the `sit` GitHub Environment only — they must not be accessible to the `production` environment workflow.\n\nOpen dependencies\n-----------------\n\n1. **NewStore notification channel** — the deploy-SIT path currently only echoes to the Actions log on success. Wire to Slack webhook, email, or Jira comment once NewStore confirms their preferred channel.\n2. **MuleSoft contract test spec source** — `test:contracts` is a placeholder. MuleSoft must publish OpenAPI specs (or expose a contract endpoint in SIT) before the contract gate is active. This is the same dependency tracked on the MuleSoft row of [7.1b Third Party Credentials](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/268337165) — update status there, not as a separate Jira item.\n3. **OMS SIT sign-off mechanism** — STAGE entry criteria requires NewStore to formally sign off OMS SIT completion on a Jira ticket. The sign-off field on that ticket type needs to be configured by the Zimmermann Jira admin.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "277708843",
    "title": "TSD: AI-Assisted Defect Triage Agent",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/277708843",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **Status:** 🟡 In Development\n> **Owner:** Integration Lead\n> **Last updated:** June 2026\n> **Registry entry:** [05.5 — Agent Registry](../278921217)\n\n---\n\n1. Purpose & Scope\n------------------\n\nThis TSD describes the architecture, component boundaries, and governance constraints for the AI-Assisted Defect Triage Agent — a hybrid agent that automates defect classification and fix-branch creation following failed BrowserStack Automate runs on the Zimmermann SFCC replatform programme.\n\n**In scope:**\n- CI-automated triage pipeline (GitHub Actions + AWS Bedrock)\n- Tier classification logic and routing decisions\n- Automated Jira defect creation and GitHub fix-branch creation\n- Claude Code CLI skill for interactive investigation and fix on Tier 1/2 defects\n- Security boundaries, credential handling, and hard stops\n\n**Out of scope:**\n- BrowserStack Automate configuration (covered in [Guide 3](../../277807105/277676053))\n- Zephyr Scale result sync (covered in Guide 3)\n- The Playwright spec authoring workflow (covered in Guide 3)\n- PR merge — this always requires a human reviewer\n\n---\n\n2. Solution Overview\n--------------------\n\nThe agent operates as a **hybrid** — a CI-automated layer handles triage and administration for all failures without any human present, and an interactive Claude Code CLI skill handles investigation and fix for Tier 1/2 defects when a developer picks up the Jira ticket.\n\nThe split is designed around risk. Tier 3/4 files are squad-owned and feature-scoped — the blast radius of an incorrect automated fix is contained and reviewable via PR. Tier 1/2 files affect all storefronts and multiple squads — automated code generation on those files introduces unacceptable risk, so the agent stops at investigation and a developer owns the fix.\n\n```\nBrowserStack run completes (failures detected)\n          │\n          ▼\nCI: GitHub Actions — defect-triage.yml\n          │\n          ├── Bedrock (Haiku): classify each failure\n          │   code defect / env issue / test data\n          │\n          ├── CODEOWNERS check: determine file tier\n          │   (deterministic — not delegated to LLM)\n          │\n          ├── Jira REST API: create defect ticket (all tiers, all classifications)\n          │\n          └── Route by tier\n                │\n                ├── Tier 3/4 code defect\n                │   ├── Bedrock (Sonnet): investigate + generate fix\n                │   ├── GitHub: create fix branch with code change\n                │   └── GitHub: raise PR (human review required)\n                │\n                ├── Tier 1/2 code defect\n                │   ├── Bedrock (Sonnet): investigate, produce root cause analysis\n                │   ├── GitHub: create fix branch (investigation notes only, no code)\n                │   └── Jira ticket pre-populated with RCA — assigned to Lead Dev (T1) or Squad Lead (T2)\n                │         │\n                │         ▼\n                │   Developer picks up ticket, runs /defect-triage [JIRA-KEY]\n                │   Claude Code CLI skill pulls context via MCP, works interactively\n                │\n                └── Env / test data issue (all tiers)\n                    └── Jira ticket raised and assigned — no branch created\n```\n\n---\n\n3. Component Architecture\n-------------------------\n\nThe agent has four components. Each has a single responsibility and a defined owner.\n\n| Component | Location | Responsibility | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **CI workflow** | `.github/workflows/defect-triage.yml` | Orchestrates the pipeline — triggers on BrowserStack failure, calls the Bedrock invocation script, drives Jira writes via the Jira REST API and GitHub actions via the GitHub API | Integration Lead |\n| **Bedrock invocation script** | `scripts/agents/defect-triage/bedrock-invoke.js` | Node.js script called by the CI workflow. Builds the prompt, calls AWS Bedrock, parses the response, returns structured JSON to the workflow | Integration Lead |\n| **CODEOWNERS tier classifier** | `scripts/agents/defect-triage/classify-tier.js` | Deterministic script — reads the affected file paths from the failure report and cross-references against CODEOWNERS to output a tier classification. No LLM involvement. | Integration Lead |\n| **Claude Code CLI skill** | `.claude/skills/defect-triage.md` | Interactive skill invoked by a developer on Tier 1/2 defects. Pulls Jira ticket context and investigation notes via the Atlassian MCP, checks out the pre-created fix branch, and guides the developer through the fix session. | Lead Developer |\n\n> **Note on MCP vs REST API boundary:** The Atlassian MCP is only available in interactive Claude Desktop sessions — it cannot be called from a GitHub Actions runner. All Jira writes from the CI workflow use the Jira REST API directly with a scoped service account token. The Atlassian MCP is used only by the Claude Code CLI skill in interactive developer sessions.\n\n---\n\n4. Tier Classification & Routing Logic\n--------------------------------------\n\nTier classification is **always deterministic**. The LLM is never asked to decide which tier a file belongs to — that decision is made by `classify-tier.js` reading CODEOWNERS. This is a hard architectural constraint: allowing the LLM to self-classify would create a path for the agent to incorrectly escalate its own permissions.\n\n```\nAffected file paths extracted from BrowserStack failure report\n          │\n          ▼\nclassify-tier.js reads CODEOWNERS\n          │\n          ├── Tier 1 match → hard stop on code generation\n          │   (design tokens, shared hooks, runtime config, CI workflows)\n          │\n          ├── Tier 2 match → investigation only, no code write\n          │   (global shell components, shared product components, hooks with 5+ consumers)\n          │\n          ├── Tier 3 match → automated fix permitted\n          │   (squad-owned components)\n          │\n          └── Tier 4 match → automated fix permitted\n              (feature-scoped files)\n```\n\n**Routing matrix by failure classification and tier:**\n\n| Classification | Tier 1 | Tier 2 | Tier 3 | Tier 4 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Code defect | Jira ticket + RCA + fix branch (notes only) → developer runs `/defect-triage` | Jira ticket + RCA + fix branch (notes only) + Tier 2 label → developer runs `/defect-triage` | Jira ticket + RCA + fix branch with code + PR (auto-fix) | Jira ticket + RCA + fix branch with code + PR (auto-fix) |\n| Environment issue | Jira ticket → assigned to relevant team | Jira ticket → assigned to relevant team | Jira ticket → assigned to relevant team | Jira ticket → assigned to relevant team |\n| Test data issue | Jira ticket → assigned to fixture owner | Jira ticket → assigned to fixture owner | Jira ticket → assigned to fixture owner | Jira ticket → assigned to fixture owner |\n\n---\n\n5. AWS Bedrock Integration\n--------------------------\n\nAll LLM calls use **AWS Bedrock**, not the Anthropic API directly. This is a programme-wide constraint — see the [Agent Registry](../278921217) governance principles.\n\n### 5.1 Model Selection\n\n| Task | Model | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Failure classification (code defect / env issue / test data) | Claude Haiku on Bedrock | Low-complexity structured classification. Fast and cost-efficient for high-volume CI runs. |\n| Root cause investigation | Claude Sonnet on Bedrock | Requires reasoning across stack traces, file contents, and test case context. Sonnet's reasoning capability justifies the higher cost at this step. |\n| Fix generation (Tier 3/4) | Claude Sonnet on Bedrock | Code generation quality matters — Haiku is not used for any code write step. |\n\n### 5.2 Invocation Pattern\n\nThe Bedrock invocation script (`bedrock-invoke.js`) uses the AWS SDK v3 `@aws-sdk/client-bedrock-runtime` package. It does not use any Anthropic SDK — all calls go through the Bedrock `InvokeModel` endpoint.\n\n```\n// bedrock-invoke.js — simplified structure\nimport { BedrockRuntimeClient, InvokeModelCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-bedrock-runtime'\n\nconst client = new BedrockRuntimeClient({ region: process.env.AWS_BEDROCK_REGION })\n\nexport async function classify(failureReport) {\n  const command = new InvokeModelCommand({\n    modelId: 'anthropic.claude-haiku-20240307-v1:0',\n    body: JSON.stringify({\n      anthropic_version: 'bedrock-2023-05-31',\n      max_tokens: 512,\n      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: buildClassificationPrompt(failureReport) }]\n    }),\n    contentType: 'application/json',\n    accept: 'application/json'\n  })\n  const response = await client.send(command)\n  return JSON.parse(new TextDecoder().decode(response.body))\n}\n\nexport async function investigate(failureReport, fileContents, testCaseDetail) {\n  const command = new InvokeModelCommand({\n    modelId: 'anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5',\n    body: JSON.stringify({\n      anthropic_version: 'bedrock-2023-05-31',\n      max_tokens: 4096,\n      messages: [{ role: 'user', content: buildInvestigationPrompt(failureReport, fileContents, testCaseDetail) }]\n    }),\n    contentType: 'application/json',\n    accept: 'application/json'\n  })\n  const response = await client.send(command)\n  return JSON.parse(new TextDecoder().decode(response.body))\n}\n```\n\n### 5.3 Credential Handling\n\nAWS credentials are stored as GitHub Actions secrets and injected as environment variables into the CI job. They are never logged, never passed in LLM prompts, and never committed to source control.\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` | Bedrock IAM access key |\n| `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | Bedrock IAM secret |\n| `AWS_BEDROCK_REGION` | AWS region hosting the Bedrock endpoint (e.g. `us-east-1`) |\n\n### 5.4 IAM Boundary\n\nThe IAM role used by the CI job must be scoped to the minimum permissions required:\n\n* `bedrock:InvokeModel` on the specific model ARNs used (Haiku and Sonnet)\n* No `bedrock:ListFoundationModels`, no `bedrock:CreateModelCustomizationJob`\n* No access to any other AWS service from this role\n\n---\n\n6. GitHub Actions Workflow Design\n---------------------------------\n\n### 6.1 Trigger\n\nThe `defect-triage.yml` workflow triggers on completion of the BrowserStack execution job in `qa.yml`, conditional on failures being present:\n\n```\non:\n  workflow_run:\n    workflows: ['BrowserStack E2E']\n    types: [completed]\n\njobs:\n  triage:\n    if: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'failure' }}\n```\n\n### 6.2 Job Structure\n\n```\njobs:\n  parse-failures        # Extract failed test names, file paths, BrowserStack session URLs\n  classify-tier         # Run classify-tier.js — deterministic CODEOWNERS check\n  classify-failures     # Call Bedrock Haiku — code defect / env / test data per failure\n  create-jira-defects   # Jira REST API — one Jira ticket per failure (service account token)\n  investigate           # Call Bedrock Sonnet — RCA for code defects\n  create-branches       # GitHub API — fix branch per code defect\n  generate-fix          # Bedrock Sonnet — code fix for Tier 3/4 only\n  raise-prs             # GitHub API — PR per Tier 3/4 fix branch\n```\n\n`classify-tier` and `classify-failures` run in parallel after `parse-failures` completes. All downstream jobs depend on both completing.\n\n### 6.3 Jira REST API Integration\n\nJira defects are created using the Jira REST API v3 with a scoped service account token. The service account must have issue creation and linking permissions in the SFCC project only — no broader Jira access.\n\n```\n// jira-create-issue.js — simplified structure\nexport async function createDefect(payload) {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    'https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue',\n    {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: {\n        'Authorization': `Basic ${Buffer.from(\n          `${process.env.JIRA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL}:${process.env.JIRA_API_TOKEN}`\n        ).toString('base64')}`,\n        'Content-Type': 'application/json'\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify(payload)\n    }\n  )\n  return response.json()\n}\n```\n\nAdditional GitHub Actions secrets required for Jira integration:\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `JIRA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL` | Email of the Jira service account used by the CI workflow |\n| `JIRA_API_TOKEN` | Jira API token scoped to issue create and link in the SFCC project |\n\n### 6.4 Jira Defect Content\n\nEach Jira defect created by the agent is pre-populated with:\n\n* **Summary:** `[STORY-KEY] [Test Case Name] — [Classification] — [Browser / Storefront]`\n* **Description:** failure message, stack trace, BrowserStack session recording link, root cause analysis (Sonnet output), affected file paths and their tier\n* **Labels:** `agent-raised`, `defect`, the classification, the storefront, the file tier\n* **Priority:** P1 if the failure blocks story sign-off; P2 otherwise\n* **Assignee:** Lead Developer (Tier 1), Squad Lead (Tier 2), unassigned (Tier 3/4 — PR raised instead)\n* **Linked to:** the originating Jira story and the Zephyr test case\n\n### 6.5 Fix Branch Naming\n\n| Tier | Branch name |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tier 1/2 (investigation only) | `fix/agent-investigation-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]` |\n| Tier 3/4 (auto-fix) | `fix/agent-autofix-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]` |\n\nThe `agent-investigation` vs `agent-autofix` prefix makes branch intent visible at a glance in the GitHub branch list and in PR titles.\n\n---\n\n7. Claude Code CLI Skill Design\n-------------------------------\n\n### 7.1 Purpose\n\nThe `/defect-triage [JIRA-KEY]` skill is invoked by a developer when they pick up a Tier 1/2 defect Jira ticket. It provides a structured, context-loaded starting point for the interactive fix session — removing the setup overhead of manually finding the failure detail, checking out the branch, and reading the investigation notes.\n\n### 7.2 What the Skill Does on Invocation\n\n```\nDeveloper runs: /defect-triage ZIM-DEF-089\n          │\n          ▼\nSkill reads JIRA-KEY from the command argument\n          │\n          ▼\nAtlassian MCP: retrieve the Jira defect ticket\n— failure detail, stack trace, BrowserStack session link\n— RCA from the CI investigation step\n— affected file paths and tier classification\n— linked Jira story and Zephyr test case\n          │\n          ▼\nGitHub MCP: check out the pre-created fix branch\nfix/agent-investigation-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[description]\n          │\n          ▼\nSkill presents a structured session brief to the developer:\n— Summary of the failure\n— The RCA the CI agent produced\n— The files involved and their tier\n— A proposed investigation approach\n— Explicit note: \"This is a Tier [1/2] file —\n  you own the fix. I will assist but will not write\n  to this file without your explicit instruction.\"\n          │\n          ▼\nDeveloper works interactively with Claude Code to\ninvestigate, implement a fix, and raise a PR\n```\n\n### 7.3 Skill Constraints\n\nThe skill observes the same tier rules as the CI agent:\n\n* Tier 1 files: Claude Code will analyse and propose a fix approach, but will not write to the file without the developer typing an explicit instruction to do so\n* Tier 2 files: Claude Code will implement a fix on developer instruction; the PR it raises will automatically carry the Tier 2 label and request a second reviewer\n* The skill will not suppress the tier warning or proceed past it silently\n\n---\n\n8. Security & Guardrails\n------------------------\n\n| Guardrail | Enforcement mechanism |\n| --- | --- |\n| No auto-merge | GitHub branch protection on `develop` and `release/**` — cannot be bypassed by any workflow or bot |\n| Tier 1 hard stop on code generation | `classify-tier.js` outputs tier before any Bedrock call; CI job conditional blocks fix generation step for Tier 1 |\n| Tier 2 auto-fix permitted but labelled | PR carries `tier-2-change` label; CODEOWNERS enforces second reviewer requirement |\n| No LLM tier classification | `classify-tier.js` is a deterministic CODEOWNERS lookup — not a Bedrock call |\n| Credentials never in LLM context | Bedrock and Jira credentials injected as CI environment variables; prompt construction never includes credential values |\n| Agent-raised PRs are identifiable | All agent PRs carry the `agent-raised` label; branch name prefix `fix/agent-` is enforced in the workflow |\n| Jira writes use a scoped service account | The CI workflow Jira service account has issue create and link permissions only — no admin, no bulk operations, no other projects |\n| CodeRabbit reviews agent PRs | `.coderabbit.yaml` applies the same review rules to agent-raised PRs as to human-raised PRs — assertive profile, tier warnings, test coverage checks |\n\n---\n\n9. Open Decisions\n-----------------\n\n| # | Decision | Status | Resolution | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OD-01 | AWS Bedrock region | 🔴 Open | `us-east-1` (lowest latency to GitHub Actions runners) vs `ap-southeast-2` (closer to Zimmermann AU operations) | Integration Lead |\n| OD-02 | IAM role provisioning | 🔴 Open | Zimmermann IT provisions the IAM role and delivers credentials to Accenture as GitHub secrets, vs Accenture provisions under a temporary sandbox account | Zimmermann IT + Integration Lead |\n| OD-03 | Jira writes in CI context — MCP vs REST API | ✅ Resolved | The Atlassian MCP requires a persistent Claude Desktop session and cannot be called from a GitHub Actions runner. **Decision: CI workflow uses the Jira REST API v3 with a scoped service account token for all automated Jira writes. The Atlassian MCP is used only by the `/defect-triage` Claude Code CLI skill in interactive developer sessions.** Registry governance principle updated to reflect this boundary. | Integration Lead |\n| OD-04 | Sonnet model version on Bedrock | 🔴 Open | Confirm which Claude Sonnet version is available in the selected Bedrock region and whether it requires a provisioned throughput commitment at expected CI run volumes | Integration Lead |\n| OD-05 | P1 vs P2 severity assignment logic | 🔴 Open | Proposal: P1 if the failing test case is tagged Critical or High priority in Zephyr; P2 otherwise | Lead QA + Integration Lead |\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
